lactobacillus and streptococcus that  will lower the overall pH to four point  four and anything this four point four  or lower will cause that bromo crease  all green to be yellow  so with that in mind let's look at the  test procedure you started out with  Snyder auger his brain initially the  Snyder auger is composed of glucose  right Lucas well we said that it's  dietary carbohydrates that these  bacteria ferment and so glucose is the  carbohydrate that's used in this test  and then I already mentioned the Roma  kriesel green that changes colors based  on the pH it's our pH indicator and then  the Snyder auger also includes auger  that's just the solidifying agent in  other words that's what keeps the stuff  in the tube kind of this solid and gel  like consistency instead of a liquid and  so here's the procedure the person will  have approximately point one milliliter  of their saliva collected this is prior  to dental care  that means prior to brushing their teeth  or they wake up first thing in the  morning and they collect a saliva sample  before they brush their teeth or floss  torben any mouthwash to collect the  bacteria that's collected in biofilms  it's kind of grown up as a biofilm on  your teeth overnight so get at least  point one milliliters of the saliva and  knock you Layton melted Snyder auger  that's just because when you order the  Snyder auger it's typically solid so you  loosen the cap boil it for a while that  will melt it then you can add your 0.1  milliliters of saliva to it but the cap  back on kind of roll it in your hands to  make the saliva is mixed throughout that  melted liquid and then as it cools to  room temperature the audible Rees  solidifying you then incubate at 37  degrees in this body temperature for  three days some protocols call for four  days but I'll tell you why that doesn't  really matter  in a second then you need to interpret  the results and you do need to be  checking the tube every 24 hours so you  can't just let 