n  particular then even if you have the  bacteria in your mouth they're not  producing this acid that will cause the  dental cavities on the other hand just  because you have a slight or negative  susceptibility does not mean that your  diet doesn't matter because you likely  still have at least a few of these if  you start eating a lot of these refined  carbohydrates they are likely to  increase in amount in your mouth which  could lead to cavities later also  remember that it's possible to not have  very many of these in your mouth and  then to suddenly get more of them in  your mouth for example if you are eating  and drinking out there people are  kissing someone anything that would  result in a lot of saliva exchange over  time you could possibly get bacteria  from their mouth that then takes up  residence in your mouth and that then  does increase your susceptibility to  cavities so if you are interested in  this you can also see some videos I have  on other differential media for example  mannitol salt agar is one that's not a  differential medium that I have a video  on that you can check out if you are  interested thanks for watching biology  professor today and have a great day
 

